Fillets and chamfers: an open contact replacing a face boundary stays in
the adjacent face's parametric period.

When a blend consumes a face, ChFi3d_ContactsAlongFaceEdges projects its
contact onto the adjacent face. On a cylinder, projection can return the
whole arc one period beyond that face's bounds. The arc still follows its
3D edge, but the resulting wire is not closed in 2D. A chamfer or fillet
as wide as a lug inside a bore then produces an unorientable face.

Apply the existing ChFi3d_PCurveInFacePeriod adjustment immediately after
assigning the projected contact. It translates only curves wholly outside
the face bounds that fit wholly inside after a whole-period shift. Closed
contacts crossing a seam retain the existing split-at-vertex handling.

--- a/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKFillet/ChFi3d/ChFi3d_Builder_0.cxx
+++ b/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKFillet/ChFi3d/ChFi3d_Builder_0.cxx
@@ -3423,6 +3423,10 @@
       const TopAbs_Orientation aTraOnF = FI.Transition();
       ChFiDS_FaceInterference& aFI = Fd->ChangeInterference(ons);
       aFI.ChangePCurveOnFace()     = PCv;
+      // Projection onto a periodic surface may place the whole contact
+      // one period beyond the adjacent face. Keep its wire closed in 2D;
+      // contacts crossing a seam are still split below.
+      ChFi3d_PCurveInFacePeriod(aFI, Fv);
       aFI.SetTransition(aTra);
       if (ons == 1)
       {
